{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "https://aureline.dev/schemas/security/m5-disclosure-history-block.schema.json",
  "title": "Aureline M5 disclosure / history block primitive: current status, affected versions / components, copy-safe CVE / GHSA reference ids, resolved-state downgrade, provenance, and open-doc / open-browser parity across Help/About, update, and support channels",
  "description": "Export-safe packet implementing Aureline's one reusable disclosure / history block primitive so advisory details, current mitigation state, disclosure or postmortem references, and resolved-state history remain inspectable without forcing users to abandon product context or lose provenance. Each disclosure-source row binds the shared block anatomy (current status, affected versions / components, copy-safe reference ids, disclosure path, provenance source, resolved-versus-active history state, and the open-doc / open-browser actions — all rendered inline without a detail drawer), the severity classes, action states, required actions, continuity claims, delivery profiles, mirror-freshness states, disclosure fields, export fields, and accessibility routes reused verbatim from the frozen M5 advisory-component matrix, the three channels (Help/About, update center, support bundle) that render the block in parity, the history states aligned field-for-field with the frozen entry_class vocabulary in schemas/security/advisory_timeline_entry.schema.json, and the focus behaviors that keep the copy-safe ids, the open actions, and the history state keyboard-reachable. Each lane carries worked resolution cases: a resolver input (one advisory's disclosure state on one disclosure-source lane with its copy-safe advisory id, optional CVE / GHSA aliases, severity, affected object, current status, history state, delivery profile, mirror freshness, disclosure path, provenance, visibility posture, action state, and continuity claim) and the resolved block — the derived display posture (a resolved / superseded / withdrawn advisory steps down to stepped_down_inspectable but keeps its current-status truth), the derived handoff posture (mirror / offline-import / external-browser sources keep provenance visible and never hand off to a dead-end link), the assembled copy-safe reference ids, the attached open-doc / open-browser / copy-ids actions, the always-inspectable and always-visible posture, the per-channel projections, and the copy-safe export summary — where the stored resolution must equal a fresh resolve of its input. Hard invariants forbid flattening the disclosure into a bare external link, hiding disclosure truth behind a detail drawer, dropping a resolved advisory out of inspectable history, hiding provenance when the source is mirrored / offline / external, and dropping the copy-safe id or export summary. The derived display and handoff postures are resolver-side vocabularies, kept out of the frozen set. The shell zone, responsive class, window class, and consumer surface vocabularies are reused verbatim from the frozen M5 shell-zone matrix; the severity class, action state, required action, continuity claim, delivery profile, mirror-freshness state, disclosure field, export field, accessibility route, qualification class, and downgrade trigger vocabularies are reused verbatim from the frozen M5 advisory-component matrix; the copy-safe reference kinds align to the frozen advisory-identity id family. Raw hostnames, raw absolute paths, raw exploit payloads, raw signatures, private registry URLs, credentials, and raw disclosure bodies never cross this boundary; only opaque export-safe reprs and copy-safe reference ids are carried. The Rust validator and resolver in crates/aureline-shell are the authoritative gate; this schema documents the shape.",
